WEBVTT

00:00:00.400 --> 00:00:08.592
Look down a long hallway in an older game. Near your feet the floor tiles are crisp. Farther away, they melt into grey mush.

00:00:08.942 --> 00:00:14.830
Switch on the setting called anisotropic filtering, and the far floor snaps back into focus.

00:00:15.180 --> 00:00:20.193
Why does a texture blur when you look along it, and what does that setting actually fix?

00:00:21.393 --> 00:00:24.487
Start with one pixel on the screen, a tiny square.

00:00:24.837 --> 00:00:34.586
Now follow it back onto the floor texture. On a floor seen at a low angle, that square lands as a long, thin shape. Long along your view, narrow across it.

00:00:34.936 --> 00:00:42.211
That shape is the pixel's footprint. It's an ellipse, and its two radii are called a, the long one, and b, the short one.

00:00:42.561 --> 00:00:52.139
The maths that turns the square into the ellipse is the Jacobian. It records how far the texture moves when you step one pixel right, and one pixel down.

00:00:53.339 --> 00:01:00.358
Games blur distant textures with mipmaps, a stack of pre shrunk copies, each half the size of the one before.

00:01:00.708 --> 00:01:07.215
The standard rule picks one blur level from the longest side of the footprint. The level is log base two of a.

00:01:07.565 --> 00:01:15.181
That blur is round. It blurs every direction by the long radius, even across the floor, where the footprint was narrow.

00:01:16.381 --> 00:01:22.503
So the mush isn't really caused by distance. It's caused by using a circle where the footprint is an ellipse.

00:01:22.853 --> 00:01:28.592
The extra blur on the short side is log base two of a over b, counted in mip levels.

00:01:28.942 --> 00:01:34.638
For a footprint eight texels long and one wide, that's three whole levels of blur nobody needed.

00:01:34.988 --> 00:01:44.012
Anisotropic filtering picks the level from the short side instead, and takes several samples along the long side. Sharp across, smooth along.

00:01:45.212 --> 00:01:51.932
On a real slanted floor, the ellipse changes from pixel to pixel. Here, colour shows how stretched it is.

00:01:52.282 --> 00:02:02.202
Near the camera it's almost a circle. Toward the horizon it stretches more and more. At this marked far pixel, it's about five times longer than it is wide.

00:02:02.552 --> 00:02:09.293
Left, the round mip blur. Right, a filter that follows the ellipse. The check edges stay sharp on the right.

00:02:10.493 --> 00:02:15.528
To measure it, we shrink a test pattern of rings, with a footprint eight long and one wide.

00:02:15.878 --> 00:02:23.195
With no blur at all, the fine rings fold into false stripes. That's aliasing, the crawling shimmer you see in games.

00:02:23.545 --> 00:02:29.156
The round mip removes the stripes, but also washes out the rings across the short side.

00:02:29.506 --> 00:02:35.863
The ellipse filter removes the stripes too, but keeps about twice as much detail across the short side.

00:02:37.063 --> 00:02:46.322
Two sanity checks. When the texture is magnified, with both radii under one, the two filters should give the same picture, and they do, exactly.

00:02:46.672 --> 00:02:55.248
And as the stretch grows, the gap between round and elliptical filtering grows with it. With no stretch at all, they're identical circles.

00:02:56.448 --> 00:03:03.744
A few limits. The ellipse filter here is a reference written for the lab, not the exact method inside graphics cards.

00:03:04.094 --> 00:03:13.331
This software renderer's own high anisotropy setting barely changed the image, so we make no claims about real hardware quality or speed.

00:03:13.681 --> 00:03:23.132
And on the full hallway picture, the difference is subtle, around one percent on average. The test pattern carries the proof. Every check in the run passed.

00:03:24.332 --> 00:03:28.513
A pixel's footprint on a slanted surface is an ellipse, not a circle.

00:03:28.863 --> 00:03:36.415
Round mip blur uses the long radius everywhere, and over blurs the short side by log two of a over b levels.

00:03:36.765 --> 00:03:43.059
Anisotropic filtering follows the ellipse. That's why turning it on rescues the far floor in games.

00:03:43.409 --> 00:03:52.987
For further reading, there's Williams on mipmaps, Greene and Heckbert on elliptical weighted average filtering, and Heckbert on the fundamentals of texture mapping.

